WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.359
Scenario: A caller claiming to be IT support says

2
00:00:03.359 --> 00:00:06.718
your laptop is infected and asks you to install

3
00:00:06.718 --> 00:00:10.077
a remote-access tool and read out an authentication code.

4
00:00:10.077 --> 00:00:13.437
Safest response: Option C: End the call and contact

5
00:00:13.437 --> 00:00:16.796
IT using the organization’s published helpdesk route. Independently verify

6
00:00:16.796 --> 00:00:20.528
support requests; never disclose authentication outputs to an unsolicited caller.

7
00:00:20.528 --> 00:00:23.887
Option A is unsafe because The code may authorize

8
00:00:23.887 --> 00:00:27.246
account access even without remote-control software. Option B is

9
00:00:27.246 --> 00:00:30.606
unsafe because Unverified remote access can give the caller

10
00:00:30.606 --> 00:00:33.965
control of the device. Option D is unsafe because

11
00:00:33.965 --> 00:00:37.324
A photo can be fabricated or copied and is

12
00:00:37.324 --> 00:00:40.683
not reliable identity verification. In practice: End the call.

13
00:00:40.683 --> 00:00:44.042
Contact the helpdesk using a known directory or portal

14
00:00:44.042 --> 00:00:47.401
and report the attempted remote-access and code request. Key

15
00:00:47.401 --> 00:00:50.761
takeaway: Caller ID, technical language and urgency are not

16
00:00:50.761 --> 00:00:53.000
proof that a caller is authorized.
